You are debugging a production issue where an internal authentication proxy server crashes with `java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space` after 72 hours of uptime. A heap dump analysis reveals millions of instances of `SessionMetadata` and `UserSession` retained in memory. The team points to the following custom caching implementation intended to automatically purge expired user sessions:

java
public class SessionRegistry {
    private final Map<UserSession, SessionMetadata> sessionCache =
            Collections.synchronizedMap(new WeakHashMap<>());

    public void registerSession(UserSession session, String clientIp) {
        SessionMetadata metadata = new SessionMetadata(session, clientIp);
        sessionCache.put(session, metadata);
    }

    public static class SessionMetadata {
        private final UserSession session;
        private final String clientIp;
        private final long loggedInTime;

        public SessionMetadata(UserSession session, String clientIp) {
            this.session = session;
            this.clientIp = clientIp;
            this.loggedInTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
        }
    }
}

You are debugging a production issue where an order fulfillment service running on Spring Boot and Hibernate inserts duplicate child records under high load. A service method annotated with `@Transactional` manipulates an existing `Order` aggregate holding a `Set<OrderItem> items = new HashSet<>()`. The `OrderItem` entity defines its `equals()` and `hashCode()` using its database surrogate key `id`:

java
@Entity
public class OrderItem {
    @Id @GeneratedValue
    private Long id;

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (this == o) return true;
        if (!(o instanceof OrderItem that)) return false;
        return id != null && id.equals(that.id);
    }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        return id != null ? id.hashCode() : 0;
    }
}


Inside the transaction, a new `OrderItem` is added to `items`, the parent `order` is saved via `orderRepository.saveAndFlush(order)`, and subsequent application code calls `items.contains(item)` to ensure the item is not processed again. The check returns `false`, causing a duplicate item to be added and flushed.
